les.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle%20of%20Aboukir%20(Lejeune)\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\"],\"depth\":3,\"links\":[\"Abu Qir\",\"Antoine-Jean Gros\",\"Artist\",\"Attack on a Large Convoy at Salinas\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Egypt\",\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\",\"French invasion of Egypt and Syria\",\"French language\",\"History painting\",\"Louis-François Lejeune\",\"Louvre\",\"Mediterranean\",\"Musée de l'Histoire de France (Versailles)\",\"Napoleon Bonaparte\",\"Oil painting\",\"Ottoman Empire\",\"Palace of Versailles\",\"Paris\",\"Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes\",\"The Battle of Aboukir\",\"The Battle of Lodi\",\"The Battle of Marengo (painting)\",\"The Battle of Moscow (painting)\",\"The Battle of Mount Tabor\",\"Versailles, Yvelines\",\"Yale University Press\",\"Template talk:1800s-painting-stub\",\"Template talk:Louis-François Lejeune\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\"},\"feedback\":0.6663831968319611},{\"t\":3,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"summary\":\"Battle of Aboukir, 27 July 1799 (French: Bataille d'Aboukir, 27 juillet 1799) is an 1804 history painting by the French artist Louis-François Lejeune. It depicts the Battle of Abukir fought on 25 July 1799 during the French invasion of Egypt. General Napoleon Bonaparte French troops defeated a force from the Ottoman Empire outside Abu Qir on the Mediterranean coast of Egypt.\\nIt was one of three paintings Lejeune exhibited at the Salon of 1804 at the Louvre, in Paris along with his The Battle of Lodi. As of 2025, it is in the collection of the Musée de l'Histoire de France at the Palace of Versailles. The 1806 painting The Battle of Aboukir by Antoine-Jean Gros is also in the collection at Versailles.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle%20of%20Aboukir%20(Lejeune)\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\"],\"depth\":3,\"links\":[\"Abu Qir\",\"Antoine-Jean Gros\",\"Artist\",\"Attack on a Large Convoy at Salinas\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Egypt\",\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\",\"French invasion of Egypt and Syria\",\"French language\",\"History painting\",\"Louis-François Lejeune\",\"Louvre\",\"Mediterranean\",\"Musée de l'Histoire de France (Versailles)\",\"Napoleon Bonaparte\",\"Oil painting\",\"Ottoman Empire\",\"Palace of Versailles\",\"Paris\",\"Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes\",\"The Battle of Aboukir\",\"The Battle of Lodi\",\"The Battle of Marengo (painting)\",\"The Battle of Moscow (painting)\",\"The Battle of Mount Tabor\",\"Versailles, Yvelines\",\"Yale University Press\",\"Template talk:1800s-painting-stub\",\"Template talk:Louis-François Lejeune\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\"},\"ladderLevel\":0.28845013529078223,\"innerBudgetRemaining\":99.50000000000003,\"probeResult\":{\"pass\":true,\"results\":[]},\"isStable\":false,\"action\":{\"type\":\"NAVIGATE\",\"title\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\"},\"next\":{\"currentTitle\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\",\"summary\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris, through the Barrière de la Villette, after his coronation. 6 June 1825 (French: Entrée de Charles X à Paris, par la barrière de la Villette, après son sacre. 6 juin 1825) is an 1825 history painting by the French artist Louis-François Lejeune. It depicts the entry of Charles X of France into his capital Paris following his coronation at Reims. The king is shown entering the city through the Villette Gate in his Coronation Coach. Today the painting is in the collection of the Palace of Versailles.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entry%20of%20Charles%20X%20into%20Paris%20After%20His%20Coronation\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\"],\"depth\":4,\"links\":[\"Artist\",\"Attack on a Large Convoy at Salinas\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"Charles X of France\",\"Coronation Coach of Charles X\",\"Coronation of Charles X\",\"French language\",\"History painting\",\"Louis-François Lejeune\",\"Oil painting\",\"Palace of Versailles\",\"Paris\",\"Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes\",\"The Battle of Lodi\",\"The Battle of Marengo (painting)\",\"The Battle of Moscow (painting)\",\"The Battle of Mount Tabor\",\"Versailles, Yvelines\",\"Template talk:1820s-painting-stub\",\"Template talk:Louis-François Lejeune\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\"},\"feedback\":-0.18505174033775829},{\"t\":4,\"state\":{\"currentTitle\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\",\"summary\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris, through the Barrière de la Villette, after his coronation. 6 June 1825 (French: Entrée de Charles X à Paris, par la barrière de la Villette, après son sacre. 6 juin 1825) is an 1825 history painting by the French artist Louis-François Lejeune. It depicts the entry of Charles X of France into his capital Paris following his coronation at Reims. The king is shown entering the city through the Villette Gate in his Coronation Coach. Today the painting is in the collection of the Palace of Versailles.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Entry%20of%20Charles%20X%20into%20Paris%20After%20His%20Coronation\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\"],\"depth\":4,\"links\":[\"Artist\",\"Attack on a Large Convoy at Salinas\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"Charles X of France\",\"Coronation Coach of Charles X\",\"Coronation of Charles X\",\"French language\",\"History painting\",\"Louis-François Lejeune\",\"Oil painting\",\"Palace of Versailles\",\"Paris\",\"Pierre-Henri de Valenciennes\",\"The Battle of Lodi\",\"The Battle of Marengo (painting)\",\"The Battle of Moscow (painting)\",\"The Battle of Mount Tabor\",\"Versailles, Yvelines\",\"Template talk:1820s-painting-stub\",\"Template talk:Louis-François Lejeune\"],\"blacklist\":[],\"lastLinkClicked\":\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\"},\"ladderLevel\":0.2699449612570064,\"innerBudgetRemaining\":99.35000000000004,\"probeResult\":{\"pass\":true,\"results\":[]},\"isStable\":false,\"action\":{\"type\":\"NAVIGATE\",\"title\":\"French language\"},\"next\":{\"currentTitle\":\"French language\",\"summary\":\"French (français [fʁɑ̃sɛ]  or langue française [lɑ̃ɡ fʁɑ̃sɛːz] ) is a Romance language of the Indo-European family. Like all other Romance languages, it descended from the Vulgar Latin of the Roman Empire. French evolved from Northern Old Gallo-Romance, a descendant of the Latin spoken in Northern Gaul. Its closest relatives are the other langues d'oïl—languages historically spoken in northern France and in southern Belgium, which French (Francien) largely supplanted. It was also influenced by native Celtic languages of Northern Roman Gaul and by the Germanic Frankish language of the post-Roman Frankish invaders. As a result of French and Belgian colonialism from the 16th century onward, it was introduced to new territories in the Americas, Africa, and Asia, and numerous French-based creole languages, most notably Haitian Creole, were developed. A French-speaking person or nation may be referred to as Francophone in both English and French.\\nFrench is an official language in 26 countries, as well as one of the most geographically widespread languages in the world, with speakers in about 50 countries. Most of these countries are members of the Organisation internationale de la Francophonie (OIF), the community of 54 member states which share the use or teaching of French. It is estimated to have about 310 million speakers, of which about 74 million are native speakers; it is spoken as a first language (in descending order of the number of speakers) in France, Canada (Quebec), Belgium (Wallonia and the Brussels-Capital Region), western Switzerland (Romandy region), parts of Luxembourg, and Monaco. Meanwhile in Francophone Africa it is spoken mainly as a second language or lingua franca, though it has also become a native language in a small number of urban areas; in some North African countries like Algeria, despite not having official status, it is also a first language among some upper classes of the population alongside the indigenous ones, but only a second one among the general population.\\nIn 2015, approximately 40% of the Francophone population (including L2 and partial speakers) lived in Europe, 36% in sub-Saharan Africa and the Indian Ocean, 15% in North Africa and the Middle East, 8% in the Americas, and 1% in Asia and Oceania. French is the second most widely spoken mother tongue in the European Union. Of Europeans who speak other languages natively, approximately one-fifth are able to speak French as a second language. Many institutions of the EU use French as a working language along with English, German and Italian; in some institutions, French is the sole working language (e.g. at the Court of Justice of the European Union). French is also the 22nd most natively spoken language in the world, the sixth most spoken language by total number of speakers, and is among the top five most studied languages worldwide, with about 120 million learners as of 2017.  French has a long history as an international language of literature and scientific standards and is a primary or second language of many international organizations including the United Nations, the European Union, the North Atlantic Treaty Organization, the World Trade Organization, the International Olympic Committee, the General Conference on Weights and Measures, and the International Committee of the Red Cross.\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French%20language\",\"goal\":\"French Revolution\",\"history\":[\"Battle of Vienna\",\"Battle of Abukir (1799)\",\"Battle of Aboukir (Lejeune)\",\"Entry of Charles X into Paris After His Coronation\",\"French language\"],\"depth\":5,\"links\":[\"1851 French coup d'état\",\"2005 French riots\",\"2017 French presidential election\",\"2021 Canadian census\",\"2021 French labor protests\",\"2022 French presidential election\",\"20 (number)\",\"AZERTY\",\"Abenaki language\",\"Abidjan\",\"Aboriginal English in Canada\",\"Abortion in France\",\"Absolute monarchy in France\",\"Acadia (region)\",\"Acadian French\",\"Académie Française\",\"Académie française\",\"Active voice\",\"Acute accent\",\"Adjective\",\"Administrative divisions of France\",\"African-American English\",\"African Court on Human and Peoples' Rights\",\"African French\",\"African Romance\",\"African Union\",\"African languages\",\"Afro-Asiatic languages\",\"Agalega creole\",\"Agence universitaire de la Francophonie\",\"Aisinian Aragonese\",\"Aisne (department)\",\"Ajië language\",\"Alemannic German\",\"Alentejan Portuguese\",\"Algeria\",\"Algerian Jewish Sign Language\",\"Algerian War\",\"Algherese dialect\",\"Algiers\",\"Algonquian languages\",\"Algonquian–Basque pidgin\",\"Algonquin language\",\"Alliance Française\",\"Alsatian dialect\",\"Alveolar consonant\",\"Amazofonia\",\"Amazon Standard Identification Number\",\"American Sign Language\",\"Amnesty International\",\"Ancien Régime\",\"Ancient Greek\",\"Ancient history\",\"Andalusi Romance\",\"Andalusian Spanish\",\"Andorra\",\"Angevin dialect\",\"Anglo-Norman language\",\"Années folles\",\"Ansó Aragonese\",\"Antananarivo\",\"Antillean Creole\",\"Aosta Valley\",\"Aostan French\",\"Appellate Body\",\"Approximant consonant\",\"Arabic\",\"Arabization\",\"Arabs\",\"Aragonese dialects\",\"Aragonese language\",\"Aragüés Aragonese\",\"Aranese dialect\",\"Argentinian Spa